My copy is PC NetHack v 3.1.0, built Feb 11 8:32:30
downloaded from linc.cis.upenn.edu

I'm still having a problem with the color display. (Kevin S. says
this is a known problem with some few systems.)  Although the 
termcap entries are the same for ANSI and NANSI as I'd used for NH
3.0j and NH3.0++, the colors in NH 3.1.0 are not working for me, I
get a grey (non-bright white) dungeon map, magenta self, magenta pet
and nearly all objects and monster are magenta, with an occasional
grey object or monster.  The full color display still works fine if I
run NH 3.0j or NH3.0++.  I have a no-name 640x480 VGA card on my 386,
and have never had any other color problems with any other software.
Setting the !nocolor,hilite_pet options seems to work as designed (and
is the only way I can tell my pets from similar monsters) so that's what I
use to play.

Many non-invisible creatures are not seen while blind(folded) with
telepathy (especially 'M' and 'F'), is that a bug or part of my above
display problem?  This is separate from the bug that makes digging
creatures disappear, as these don't reappear on a screen-redraw.

As long as I'm blindfolded, Medusa gets turned to stone as soon as I
enter her room, although telepathy shows she wasn't by the door.  Is this 
a bug, or is my shield of reflection killing her?  If it is the shield,
why do I get turned to stone if I'm not blindfolded?

There seems to be some kind of problem in the game's distinguishing
intrinsic teleport from the spell of teleport away.  Using ctrl-T
depletes the teleport away spell count, and when the spell is used
up, gives spell-related messages and causes confusion, although I
could still teleport.

I thought blessed scrolls of identify were supposed to identify more
than one item, but many identify scrolls that I blessed by dipping in
holy water still only identified one item. 

(To the best of my memory, this is consistent with earlier versions,
but,) rings and potions of invisibility still only seem to be
annoying for the player to find the cursor, while they have no
appreciable effect on monster attacks (as if nearly all monster had
intrisic 'see invisible'), although cloaks and intrisic invisibility
cause monsters to miss you on attacks. (though they still seem to
sense you too much, I think it ought to approach stealth in effect,
unless your dexterity is poor or you're overburdened, so you'd be
loud/clumsy) BTW, is there any way to get rid of intrinsic
invisibility other than getting it stolen by gremlins?  It's a big
pain getting snubbed by shopkeepers.

The status line in 3.1 shows the same dungeon level numbers for the
gnomish mine levels as the non-mine levels (e.g., if the first mine
level is off dungeon level 3, it shows as level 4 on the status
line).  How would I specify which is which on a level-transport with
teleport control?

I noticed that on starting a save file, it states the current dungeon
level as the farthest level you reached, while in 3.0j, if you had come
back up from a deeper level, it stated the actual deepest level you'd
visited.

In the distant past in either early (unix) Hack or perhaps Rogue,
there was an option "flush-type-ahead" on combat, so one didn't
get slugged to death after having held down a key like 's'earch.  I
hope the development team will consider putting that option back in. 
I get way too many stupid-deaths that way (and it's why I back up save
files).

Does polypiling piss off the gods now?  I had just sacrificed enough
corpses to receive several clovers and gift at my feet, had a blessed
luckstone, and had even thrown a few identified minor gems (amber) to
an co-aligned unicorn, but after using a polymorph spell a few times
on some piles of stuff, I starved to death, because my god was
displeased when I prayed in hopes of his fixing my hunger.

I think completing a quest ought to please one's god.  I prayed over
water on the altar right after bringing back the Mitre of Holiness to
the temple, and my god was displeased!!!   Meanwhile, in the first 25
levels, the only attended co-aligned altar that I found was the
temple, and nobody would ask for a contribution there.  I sacrificed
like crazy, had a blessed luckstone, showed intrinsic extremely lucky,
and had eight darn thrones poof away without any wishes at all --
doesn't sound particularly lucky to me!!  

Having spellbooks hang around after reading is kind of nice, but they
seem to be awfully heavy to drag around for a book containing one
lousy spell (don't these things ever get published in paperback?),
and the better spells get used up way too fast -- one or two uses.
I thought there was supposed to be some number related to the spell
level and character level.  I prefer the old vanishing books.

